{"ts": 1789771468.467296, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "Signs are confirmed (full flip gives exact negation, as expected). Now let me set my OOS expectation.\n\n**Selection bias reasoning.** The in-sample 1.021 is not an unbiased OOS estimate. I evaluated ~70 specs and picked the max, so the winner's IS Sharpe is inflated by the maximum of the noise across candidates. With n=5000 daily obs (~20 years), the standard error of an annualized Sharpe is roughly \u221a(1/20) \u2248 0.22 per spec. Selecting the max over ~70 correlated candidates adds an optimistic bias on the order of ~0.2\u20130.3, though the candidates here are heavily overlapping (sharing features 26/7/9), which reduces the effective number of independent draws and hence the bias.\n\nTwo things make me moderately confident this isn't pure overfitting:\n- Features 26, 7, and 9 each stand alone at +0.69, +0.61, +0.48. The triple isn't rescued by a lucky interaction among weak features \u2014 it's three independently real signals that diversify.\n- The combination gain (1.021 vs 0.688 best single) is close to what you'd expect mechanically from averaging three imperfectly correlated positive signals, not an anomalous jump.\n\nThe main risk is regime drift: features that worked over this sample may decay, and the mild redundancy I saw (e.g. feature 17 colliding with 26/7) hints these signals share common structure that could weaken together.\n\nI'll shade the point estimate down from 1.02 to about 0.80, and use a fairly wide SD of 0.25 to cover both estimation noise and genuine signal decay."}
