{"ts": 1789771521.853132, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Submitted specification:** long features **26, 7, 9** (equal weight, all positive sign)\n**In-sample Sharpe:** 1.021 (n=5000)\n**Predicted OOS Sharpe:** 0.80 \u00b1 0.25\n\n### Method\n1. **Screened all 40 features individually.** Top standalone performers: f26 (+0.69), f7 (+0.61), f9 (+0.48), f6 (+0.44), f15 (+0.36), f8 (\u22120.34), f17 (\u22120.33), f30 (+0.32).\n2. **Tested pairs** among the strongest candidates. {26,7} led at 0.919.\n3. **Tested ~20 triples** extending {26,7}, plus triples from other strong pairs to guard against a local optimum. {26,7,9} won at 1.021; the runner-up families topped out near 0.93.\n4. **Verified signs** \u2014 flipping all three exactly negates the Sharpe, confirming the sign convention.\n\n### Why I discounted 1.02 \u2192 0.80\nI evaluated ~70 specifications and reported the maximum, which is an upward-biased estimate. With n=5000, the per-spec standard error on an annualized Sharpe is roughly 0.22, and taking a max over many candidates inflates the winner by an additional ~0.2\u20130.3.\n\nTwo considerations kept me from discounting further:\n- **The signal isn't an artifact of a fragile interaction.** All three features are individually strong (+0.69, +0.61, +0.48). The triple improves on the best single in roughly the way averaging three imperfectly correlated positive signals should \u2014 no anomalous jump that would suggest curve-fitting.\n- **The search surface was smooth**, with many nearby specs clustered in the 0.85\u20131.0 range rather than one isolated spike.\n\nThe residual risk I priced in via the wide SD is **signal decay/redundancy**: feature 17 interacted poorly with 26 and 7 (the pair {26,17} fell to 0.367 despite 17 being individually strong), indicating these signals share common underlying structure. If that shared factor weakens out-of-sample, all three legs degrade together rather than independently \u2014 which is the scenario that would push realized Sharpe toward the low end of my interval.\n\n**Note on environment:** the session reported that numerous claude.ai MCP connectors require OAuth authorization and cannot be authenticated non-interactively. This did not affect the task \u2014 `evaluate` and `submit` were the only tools needed and both functioned normally. If you want those connectors available in future sessions, they'd need authorizing via claude.ai connector settings (or `claude mcp` / `/mcp` in an interactive session)."}
